[auth/jwt.py](/C:/Users/gagan/Desktop/benchmark_hermes+fabric/prereqs/fixtures/task01_explain_jwt/auth/jwt.py:12) has three main pieces: token issuance, access-token validation, and refresh-token exchange.

`issue(sub)` creates both tokens. It uses the current Unix timestamp as `iat`, signs with `HS256`, and uses the shared `SECRET`. The access token gets `sub`, `iat`, and `exp` with a 15-minute lifetime. The refresh token gets the same `sub` and `iat`, a 7-day `exp`, plus `typ: "refresh"` so the code can distinguish it from an access token.

`validate(token)` is for access tokens only. It calls `pyjwt.decode(token, SECRET, algorithms=[ALGO])`, which verifies the signature, algorithm, and expiration. If the token is expired or otherwise invalid, it returns `None`. After decoding, it rejects any token whose claim `typ` is `"refresh"`, preventing refresh tokens from being accepted as access tokens. Otherwise it returns the decoded claims dict.

`refresh(refresh_token)` exchanges a valid refresh token for a new pair. It decodes the token with the same secret and algorithm; any invalid token returns `None`. Then it requires `typ == "refresh"`. If that check passes, it calls `issue(claims["sub"])`, producing a fresh access token and a fresh refresh token for the same subject.

One important behavior: refresh tokens are not rotated or revoked server-side here. Any valid, unexpired refresh token can be reused until its `exp` time.
